INSERT INTO research_companies (slug, legal_name, jurisdiction, operational_hq, primary_archetype, self_described_archetype, reporting_standard, functional_currency, presentation_currency, financial_year_end, tier, expected_disclosure_cadence, market_cap_band, funding_source, acn, abn, arbn, isin, lei, curator_notes, last_verified_at, is_published)
SELECT
  '333d',
  '333D Limited',
  'AU',
  NULL,
  'treasury_allocation',
  'treasury_allocation',
  'aasb',
  'AUD',
  'AUD',
  '06-30',
  'peer_shaped',
  'episodic',
  NULL,
  'operating_cash',
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  $q$The closest thing in the register to an actual BTS client: a small ASX-quoted operating company — digital asset management and 3D printing services, cash receipts just over A$1m for FY2025 — that put surplus cash into bitcoin under a board-approved policy. Everything above it in the register is a treasury company at a scale no mid-market Australian CFO will ever occupy. This one is the shape of the decision they are actually making, and it is the reason the tier is peer_shaped rather than regional.

The reason to read it: the exchange intervened. Secondary accounts describe a trading halt on 27 August 2025 in response to ASX queries, a voluntary suspension from 28 August, an extension while ASX reviewed whether the bitcoin treasury activities constituted a significant change in the nature or scale of the company's operations, a response arguing that approving the policy was not market-sensitive and committed the company to nothing, and resumption in early September. That is Listing Rule 11.1 being applied to a treasury policy rather than to an acquisition, and it is the concrete form of the risk that Locate resolved by leaving the ASX altogether. Two records, one exchange, two outcomes: one company left, one was suspended and stayed. For an Australian operating company weighing this, that pair is the whole lesson — the question is not whether you may hold bitcoin, it is whether doing so turns you into a different company in the exchange's eyes.

None of that sequence is stored as fact. Every account of it sighted here is a news summary of an announcement rather than the announcement itself, which is rank 6 against the rank 2 a listing change requires. The announcements exist; they were not located in this pass. This is the register's first refusal caused by retrieval failure rather than by the quality of the underlying source, and the distinction matters: the fix is a document URL, not a better source.

Quantity trap, and a new one: no bitcoin quantity has been disclosed anywhere sighted. The February 2026 update reports a market value of A$228,838 and an impairment of A$141,661, being 13.6% of consolidated total assets — the threshold that triggered the continuous disclosure obligation — with no unit count at all. A holdings disclosure denominated purely in local currency cannot produce a snapshot, cannot carry a basis, and cannot enter any aggregate. Worse for interpretation, the company attributes the decline to both bitcoin price movement and a stronger Australian dollar, and states that it hedges neither exposure, so the single number conflates an asset price change with an FX change. An AUD-denominated bitcoin position is two positions.

Watch the word "impairment" against the Metaplanet record. Here it is an AASB reporter using it about an intangible, where impairment genuinely is one-way. There it is a translation of a symmetric Japanese GAAP remeasurement. Same word, opposite mechanics, in two records filed a fortnight apart. Whichever way the source-class decision goes, the classifier will meet both.

Identity left blank on purpose: two secondary profiles give different head offices (Sydney and Melbourne), so operational_hq is null rather than guessed, and market_cap_band is null for the same reason — the figures range from about A$4.6m to A$25m across undated secondary snapshots.

Gaps: the accounting basis for the holding is not sourced (the "impairment" language reaches this record only through news summaries); no custody disclosure; no acquisition announcement located, so the policy exists in the ledger but the purchases it governs do not; the ASX suspension sequence needs its primary announcements. The August 2025 announcement does confirm the ASX PDF URL shape, which is the same thing Locate needs.$q$,
  '2026-09-23T00:00:00+00:00',
  FALSE
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM research_companies WHERE slug = '333d');

