  'Tokyo, Japan',
  'treasury_company',
  'treasury_company',
  'other',
  'JPY',
  'JPY',
  '12-31',
  'bellwether',
  'quarterly',
  NULL,
  'mixed',
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  $q$The register's first non-Anglophone record, and the first where the authoritative text is a translation. Disclosure runs through TDnet, the Tokyo exchange's timely disclosure system; the company posts English versions of each notice on its own site. Everything stored here comes from those English versions, which is a provenance qualification the other four records do not carry.

Translation trap, and the most expensive thing on this page for an Australian reader: the English notices call the period-end write-down a "Bitcoin impairment loss". It is not impairment in the AASB 136 sense — one-way, non-reversible, no write-back. Under Japanese GAAP the holding is marked to market at each quarter-end with the movement taken to non-operating income or expense, symmetrically, so the same line reverses upward when the price recovers. The January 2026 notice says so plainly ("marked its Bitcoin holdings to market at each quarter-end"), then uses the impairment label in its own title. A CFO who maps the English word to the Australian standard draws exactly the wrong conclusion about what happens on the way back up. The same wording recurs for Q1 FY2026 (about ¥116.4bn by secondary accounts).

Source-ladder inversion, and it settles an argument. In Japan the quarterly results and earnings-forecast revisions ARE exchange announcements — TDnet timely disclosures at rank 2 — so accounting treatment is storable for this record. The identical class of fact is refused for Block and for Strategy, because in the US it arrives in a 10-K, which the ladder ranks at 3. Same fact, same quality of evidence, opposite outcome, decided entirely by which country's filing system carried it. That is the clearest argument yet for ranking by filing item rather than by document.

Comparability trap against Strategy: Metaplanet's aggregate cost basis is stated "net of all fees and expenses". Strategy's is stated inclusive of fees. The two average-cost figures are not the same measurement, and any cross-record cost comparison silently mixes them. Ledger rows here carry fees_included = false; Strategy's carry true.

Ingest trap: every quarterly notice republishes the entire holdings history back to 23 April 2024 — about sixty rows restated in full each time. An ingest that treats the table as new rows will duplicate the whole ledger on every run. This is the best available test of reconcile.ts idempotency, better than Locate, because the restatement is the document's normal shape rather than an exception.

Structural feature worth the record on its own: the 27th Series stock acquisition rights, resolved 16 March 2026, may only be exercised while the company's mNAV is at or above 1.01. Dilution is switched off below parity by the terms of the instrument. The existing 23rd and 24th Series were suspended in connection with it. The mechanism is storable; the mNAV figure itself stays restricted.

Company-defined KPIs, all restricted: BTC Yield, BTC Gain, BTC ¥ Gain, "effective net acquisition cost" and mNAV. Two are worth understanding even though they can never be published. BTC ¥ Gain is computed with a single fixed reference price (¥10,610,570) applied across every quarter, so it is not a market value of anything. "Effective net acquisition cost" nets options revenue against the purchase price — an economic claim about the treasury strategy, not a cost measurement.

Schema: reporting_standard has no value for Japanese GAAP, so this record reads "other" — the only one of five where the standard field says nothing useful. The financial year ends 31 December, but the company's own year-end holdings row is dated 30 December.

Gaps: no custody disclosure located in this pass. The Q2 FY2026 notice (2,823 BTC, 43,000 total at 30 June 2026) was not retrieved — only secondary reports of it, so neither the event nor the snapshot is stored. The OTCQX line is secondary-sourced. A former name is widely reported and is not recorded, because nothing sighted here states it.$q$,
  '2026-09-23T00:00:00+00:00',
  FALSE
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM research_companies WHERE slug = 'metaplanet');

