-- ============================================================
-- Panther Metals PLC (panther-metals)
-- Last verified: 2026-09-23T00:00:00+00:00
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO research_companies (slug, legal_name, jurisdiction, operational_hq, primary_archetype, self_described_archetype, reporting_standard, functional_currency, presentation_currency, financial_year_end, tier, expected_disclosure_cadence, market_cap_band, funding_source, acn, abn, arbn, isin, lei, curator_notes, last_verified_at, is_published)
SELECT
  'panther-metals',
  'Panther Metals PLC',
  'IM',
  NULL,
  'treasury_allocation',
  'treasury_allocation',
  'ifrs',
  'GBP',
  'GBP',
  NULL,
  'peer_shaped',
  'episodic',
  NULL,
  'equity_issuance',
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  $q$The gap between the announcement and the balance sheet, which is the thing this register exists to measure.

On 23 June 2025 Panther announced it had opened a bitcoin treasury account with CoinCorner in the Isle of Man, planned to buy £4m of bitcoin funded by warrant conversions and a capital raise, and intended to pledge about £1.3m of it as collateral for a loan to acquire the Pick Lake deposit in Ontario. The shares rose about 22% on the day. The following day it announced the purchase of one bitcoin — roughly £78,000 at the time, about one fiftieth of the programme — described as the first in a series, with no price disclosed. Nothing sighted since, through September 2026, discloses a further purchase, a holdings total, or whether the collateralised loan was ever drawn. Fourteen months of announcements about drilling and assays, and none about the treasury.

That is not an accusation of anything; plans change, and a capital raise that does not complete is an ordinary outcome for a company this size. It is the point of the record. A CFO reading the June 2025 coverage would have understood Panther to be a £4m bitcoin holder. The register's job is to answer "what does it actually hold" and, here, the honest answer is one bitcoin, sourced only to a news report, and no figure disclosed since.

Nothing is in the ledger as a result. The one purchase reaches this record through a secondary news report, which is rank 6 against the rank 2 a ledger event requires, so there is no event and no snapshot. The register's first company where the entire disclosed holding fails the gate — Block and DigitalX had figures that could not be stored for want of a basis; this one has a figure that cannot be stored for want of a primary source. Both refusals look identical on the page and mean completely different things, which is an argument for recording why a record is empty, not just that it is.

What did store is the register's first custody fact, and it is faintly absurd that it comes from the smallest holding: Block's custodians and Strategy's custodians are both refused because they sit in 10-Ks at rank 3, while Panther naming CoinCorner in an RNS clears the bar at rank 2. One bitcoin with documented custody arrangements, hundreds of thousands of bitcoin without. The gate is measuring the filing system, not the diligence.

Bitcoin as collateral, which no other record does. The stated model is bitcoin as "productive capital" — pledged to secure a loan rather than held passively — and the company said it did not intend to borrow further against the reserve beyond that. If executed, the holding would be encumbered, and the schema has no way to say so: holding_bases offers direct_spot, look_through, includes_customer_assets and stated_unreconciled, and pledged bitcoin is direct spot in every respect except the one that matters to a creditor. A fifth basis, or an encumbrance flag, is the modelling question this record raises. Locate's lender covenant and Hamak's sale-to-fund-operations both point the same way: for small companies the interesting question is not how much bitcoin is held but what claims sit in front of it.

Also unstored: a July 2025 proposal to let bitcoin holders subscribe for new shares with bitcoin, converted to sterling at settlement with the company repurchasing on-market, and talk of a regulatory base in Dubai. Both are intentions rather than events.

Identity: incorporated in the Isle of Man, company number 009753V, per the RNS letterhead — which makes this the register's second UK-listed, non-UK-incorporated issuer after Hamak, and the second time in two records that "UK company" would have been wrong.

Freshness: the most recent treasury disclosure sighted is July 2025. On an episodic cadence this record reads stale by more than a year, and that staleness is the most informative thing on the page.

Gaps: financial year end unknown; no accounting treatment sighted; no confirmation the capital raise, the collateralised loan or the Pick Lake completion occurred; the 24 June 2025 purchase needs its RNS.$q$,
  '2026-09-23T00:00:00+00:00',
  FALSE
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM research_companies WHERE slug = 'panther-metals');

